* Frozen netlist snapshot for record 20260801-070046-50bffb1
* source     : sim/track-switch-sampling/testbench/tb_track_sampling.spice
* sha256     : 94be55b68488a1d7d5cf66d1a5da1b768f6c7d8bd805fa63ae6f57bdef3f9420
* This is a verbatim copy taken at record time. Do not edit.
* tb_track_sampling -- sampled-mode behaviour of the input sampling switch
*
* Netlist FRAGMENT. The harness supplies models, corner .lib sections, .temp,
* the .control block and .end. Harness parameters used: vdd_val, temp_c, and
* the PDK-variant-bound mim_cap_2f0 wrapper subckt.
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* WHAT THIS MEASURES, AND WHY IT IS SEPARATE FROM sim/track-switch-thd/
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* sim/track-switch-thd/ measures the one error mechanism that exists while the
* switch is CLOSED and the input is moving (R_on-modulated tracking lag). This
* testbench measures everything that happens around the switch OPENING, with
* the real duty cycle, the real load, and the real turn-off edge:
*
*   1. ACQUISITION SETTLING -- from a worst-case full-scale step, how much
*      error is left at the end of the 300 ns track window. The prior-art
*      survey (Sec 5.2) asserts this is a non-issue at >30 tau; the issue
*      requires it confirmed, not assumed, so it is measured at every corner.
*   2. CHARGE INJECTION / CLOCK FEEDTHROUGH, with the CDAC array as the actual
*      load rather than an idealised cap, across the ratiometric input range.
*      What matters is not the pedestal itself (a constant pedestal is an
*      offset, spec'd at <= 2 LSB and digitally removable) but its
*      INPUT-DEPENDENT part, which splits into
*         - a term LINEAR in V_in  -> gain error (spec row: <= 0.5 LSB), and
*         - the residual nonlinear part -> distortion / INL.
*      Both are extracted here, separately, because they are compared against
*      different spec rows.
*   3. HOLD DROOP over the 700 ns conversion phase from off-state leakage,
*      worst at 125 C.
*
* A FOURTH item -- DEVICE TERMINAL-VOLTAGE STRESS inside the bootstrap -- was
* originally in scope here (every terminal pair of the ten bootstrap devices,
* reduced to one worst-case number over the whole PVT grid) but the bs10/bs20
* branches below are DISABLED (commented out) as of DR-0007
* (spec/decision-records/DR-0007-track-switch-topology.md): that record
* rejects the bootstrap topology on two grounds independent of this
* testbench's own output -- (a) sim/track-switch-thd already shows the chosen
* 4x T-gate clears the SFDR floor without it, and (b) a real bootstrap's
* boosted node reaches up to 6.6 V, a categorical overstress of the 3.3 V
* device flavor (DR-0004) -- so no simulated number here could change that
* decision. Measured directly (2026-07-31, tt/27c/3.30v, single point,
* isolated from the rest of this deck): the "boot" subckt's internal
* positive-feedback turn-on loop (M3/M5/M6 below) costs the ngspice transient
* solver >10x the CPU time of the other 20 branches in this deck COMBINED --
* the non-bootstrap branches alone converge in ~7.5 s of CPU time, while the
* full 27-branch deck (with bs10/bs20) did not converge even after >100 CPU
* seconds under the same conditions, i.e. the bs10/bs20 branches dominate a
* multi-hundred-CPU-second full-grid run rather than sharing it evenly across
* 27 branches. Re-enabling them (uncomment "===== bs10 ====="
* and "===== bs20 =====" below, and the matching tb.json meas/let/measure
* entries in git history at this file's prior revision) is a legitimate
* follow-up if the bootstrap alternative is ever revisited -- the subckt
* definition itself is left intact for exactly that reason.
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* TIMING
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* 1 MS/s: 1 us period, 300 ns track / 700 ns hold (spec/prior-art-survey.md
* Sec 1.4, DR-0003). Two cycles are run; cycle 1 primes, cycle 2 is measured,
* so no measurement depends on the DC operating point. External clock edges
* are 1 ns (16 MHz system clock, DR-0003). The bootstrap's own turn-off edge
* is NOT the external edge -- its gate node collapses through its internal
* cascoded pull-down -- which is one of the things being measured.
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* RATIOMETRIC FULL SCALE
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Input levels are f * vdd_val, f = 0, 1/4, 1/2, 3/4, 1 -- i.e. V_REF = V_DD
* at every corner, per README#target-specification note [c] (full scale is
* ratiometric to V_REF, V_REF <= V_DD) and the same convention
* sim/device-switch-ron/ and sim/device-switch-charge-injection/ used. A fixed
* 3.3 V input at the 2.97 V corner is out of spec and would measure a
* forward-biased junction, not a switch.
*
* BOTH INPUT MODES come out of this one ladder by arithmetic, no extra
* branches: in single-ended (pseudo-differential) mode the reference side
* samples V_cm, so the differential sampling error is ped(f) - ped(f50); in
* true differential mode the mirror side samples the complementary level, so
* it is ped(f) - ped(1-f).
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* LOAD
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* 8.83 pF per side = 512 x C_u at C_u = 17.24 fF (DR-0006 /
* spec/cdac-sizing-memo.md Sec 5.2), as a real MiM cap so the mimcap process
* corners stay live. Source impedance 500 ohm, DR-0001's contracted worst
* case -- load-bearing here, because the injected channel charge splits
* between the driven source and the floating hold node in a ratio that
* depends on it.
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* CONFIGURATIONS UNDER TEST
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*  tg1     CMOS T-gate, NMOS 10u / PMOS 20u (sim/device-switch-ron/ geometry)
*  tg4     the same T-gate upsized 4x -- the size sim/track-switch-thd/ shows
*          is needed to clear SFDR >= 62 dB. Its charge injection is the other
*          half of that trade and is what this testbench prices.
*  tg4dum  tg4 plus dummy half-switches on the hold node clocked with the
*          complementary phase -- the survey's default compensation scheme
*          (spec/prior-art-survey.md Sec 5.3), given its fair chance here.
*  s10/s20/s30/s50/s150
*          tg4dum's exact topology (rd = 1:2) re-run at source impedances
*          of 10/20/30/50/150 ohm instead of DR-0001's contracted 500 ohm
*          worst case, to separate the dummy's sizing from the drive
*          condition that sets the turn-off charge split (#39).
*  d70     rd = 0.70 dummy at 500 ohm.
*  d85     rd = 0.85 dummy at 500 ohm -- the ratio that nulls the
*          input-dependent pedestal at the CONTRACTED source impedance.
*  cx100p/cx100pl, cx1n/cx1nl
*          rd = 1:2 dummy PLUS a 100 pF / 1 nF external shunt capacitor at
*          the input pin, each measured at Rs = 500 ohm (cx100p, cx1n) and
*          Rs = 25 ohm (cx100pl, cx1nl). Controlled pairs: rd and C_ext
*          fixed, only Rs moves, so the pair difference isolates how much
*          drive dependence survives the shunt capacitor (#39).
*  cxopt   the candidate compliant configuration: 25 ohm + 1 nF external
*          drive network AND rd re-tuned to 7/16 = 0.4375 (dummy
*          17.5u/35.0u) for the charge split that network pins (#39).
*  cxopth/cxoptl
*          the same candidate configuration at Rs = 500 ohm and Rs = 1 ohm
*          -- the two ends of DR-0001's allowed source-impedance range --
*          which is what decides whether a single fixed dummy ratio is
*          defensible for a part a user drives however they like (#39).
*  bs10    bootstrapped switch, sampling device 10u -- DISABLED, see above
*  bs20    bootstrapped switch, sampling device 20u -- DISABLED, see above
*
* NOT MODELLED: bottom-plate sampling. DR-0006 ratifies TOP-plate sampling for
* the MCS/Vcm array (it is what makes the MSB free), so the delayed-turn-off
* ground switch the survey lists as the primary charge-injection remedy is not
* available to this design. That is why compensation here has to come from the
* switch itself.


* ===========================================================================
* BOOTSTRAPPED SWITCH (gf180mcu 3.3 V devices)
* ===========================================================================
* Topology after Abo & Gray 1999 (the terminal-voltage-limited bootstrap),
* re-derived here for gf180mcu 3.3 V devices and independently sized. The
* sky130 10-bit SAR (UAH-IC-Design-Team, Apache-2.0) that
* spec/prior-art-survey.md Sec 5.1 cites as a portable reference schematic
* uses the same device-level arrangement at 1.8 V; nothing is copied from it,
* and its 1.8 V sizing does not transfer.
*
* Operation, phi = track (clk high), phib = hold (clk low):
*
*   phib: M1 grounds the boost cap's bottom plate n3; M4 (gate = n4 = 0)
*         charges its top plate n2 to VDD, so C_B holds exactly V_DD.
*         M2 holds n1 at VDD, which is M5's gate -- with M5's source also at
*         VDD that is V_sg = 0, so the link to the switch gate is off.
*         M10 pulls n5 low and M9 (gate at VDD) carries that down to n4, so
*         the sampling device's gate is at ground and the switch is off.
*
*   phi:  M3 discharges n1 to n3 (= 0 at the start of the phase), which turns
*         M5 on and dumps n2 onto the gate node n4. n4 = VDD is already enough
*         to turn on M7, which pulls n3 up toward V_in; n2 = n3 + V_DD rides
*         with it and n4 follows, so the loop settles at n4 = V_in + V_DD.
*         M3 alone cannot hold n1 = n3 once n3 approaches V_DD (threshold
*         loss), which is exactly why M6 exists: its gate is the boosted node,
*         so it completes n1 = n3 with no threshold drop. M8 -- the sampling
*         device -- therefore sees V_gs = V_DD at EVERY input level. That is
*         the whole point: R_on stops modulating with the input, and the
*         channel charge stops modulating with it too.
*
* WHY M9 IS A CASCODE AND NOT A PLAIN PULL-DOWN. n4 reaches V_in + V_DD. If
* M10 were tied straight to it, M10 would stand off 2*V_DD drain-source. M9's
* gate is held at V_DD, so n5 self-charges to V_DD - V_th and then M9 turns
* off, splitting the 2*V_DD between two devices. M9 is drawn at L = 0.5 um
* rather than the 0.28 um minimum for that reason: it is the one device in
* the circuit whose V_ds still exceeds V_DD (by V_th, while carrying no
* current), and channel length is the cheap defence. This is measured, not
* assumed -- see the vds_casc / vstress measurements.
*
* Bodies: M4 and M5 have their n-wells tied to n2 (the boosted node), never
* to VDD, so no source/drain-body junction is ever forward-biased when n2
* rises above V_DD.
.subckt boot vin vout clk clkb vdd wsw=20u
XM1  n3  clkb 0    0   nfet_03v3 w=8u  l=0.28u
XM2  n1  clk  vdd  vdd pfet_03v3 w=4u  l=0.28u
XM3  n1  clk  n3   0   nfet_03v3 w=4u  l=0.28u
XCB  n2  n3   mim_cap_2f0 c_width=22.18u c_length=22.18u
XM4  n2  n4   vdd  n2  pfet_03v3 w=8u  l=0.28u
XM5  n4  n1   n2   n2  pfet_03v3 w=16u l=0.28u
XM6  n1  n4   n3   0   nfet_03v3 w=4u  l=0.28u
XM7  vin n4   n3   0   nfet_03v3 w=8u  l=0.28u
XM8  vin n4   vout 0   nfet_03v3 w='wsw' l=0.28u
XM9  n4  vdd  n5   0   nfet_03v3 w=8u  l=0.5u
XM10 n5  clkb 0    0   nfet_03v3 w=8u  l=0.28u
.ends

* ---- transmission gate, plain -------------------------------------------
.subckt tgate vin vout clk clkb vdd wn=10u wp=20u
XN vin clk  vout 0   nfet_03v3 w='wn' l=0.28u
XP vin clkb vout vdd pfet_03v3 w='wp' l=0.28u
.ends

* ---- transmission gate + dummy half-switches ----------------------------
* The dummies' source and drain are shorted onto the hold node and their
* gates take the opposite phase, so the channel charge they absorb on turn-ON
* is meant to cancel the charge the main devices release on turn-OFF. It
* addresses CHANNEL charge only: gate-overlap clock feedthrough is injected
* by both the main and the dummy device and does not cancel by this
* construction.
*
* rd is the DUMMY-TO-MAIN WIDTH RATIO. rd = 0.5 (the default, and the ratio
* design/track-switch/track_switch.sch carries) is the textbook value: it
* assumes the main device's channel charge divides EVENLY between its two
* terminals on turn-off, so a dummy of half the width -- which draws its
* whole channel from the hold node, both terminals being on it -- absorbs
* exactly the half that landed there. That even split is an assumption about
* the SOURCE IMPEDANCE, not about the devices: it only holds for a hard
* (ideal-voltage-source) drive. #39 sweeps rd, and Rs, to measure where the
* null actually is.
.subckt tgate_dum vin vout clk clkb vdd wn=40u wp=80u rd=0.5
XN  vin  clk  vout 0   nfet_03v3 w='wn' l=0.28u
XP  vin  clkb vout vdd pfet_03v3 w='wp' l=0.28u
XDN vout clkb vout 0   nfet_03v3 w='wn*rd' l=0.28u
XDP vout clk  vout vdd pfet_03v3 w='wp*rd' l=0.28u
.ends


vsup vdd 0 dc {vdd_val}

* ---- 1 MS/s sampling clock: 300 ns track, 700 ns hold, 1 ns edges ----
vclk  nclk  0 pulse(0 {vdd_val} 0 1n 1n 299n 1u)
vclkb nclkb 0 pulse({vdd_val} 0 0 1n 1n 299n 1u)

* ---- ratiometric DC input ladder (V_REF = vdd_val) -------------------
vin_f00 nin_f00 0 dc 0
vin_f25 nin_f25 0 dc {vdd_val/4}
vin_f50 nin_f50 0 dc {vdd_val/2}
vin_f75 nin_f75 0 dc {3*vdd_val/4}
vin_f100 nin_f100 0 dc {vdd_val}

* ---- worst-case acquisition step: 0 V sampled in cycle 1, full scale
*      applied during cycle 1's hold, so cycle 2 must acquire the whole
*      range from the opposite rail inside one 300 ns track window.
vin_step nin_step 0 pwl(0 0 699n 0 700n {vdd_val})

* ===== tg1 =================================================================
Rs_tg1_f00 nin_f00 ni_tg1_f00 500
Xsw_tg1_f00 ni_tg1_f00 no_tg1_f00 nclk nclkb vdd tgate wn=10u wp=20u
XCl_tg1_f00 no_tg1_f00 0 mim_cap_2f0 c_width=66.36u c_length=66.36u
